    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with a high-ceiling core anchored by **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (81.59) and **Gunnar Henderson** (78.37), both of whom are set to capitalize on favorable matchups against sub-elite arms. **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** thrives against the soft-tossing Anderson, while **Gunnar Henderson** benefits from a high-park-factor environment against Perkins, with his platoon advantage effectively negating the moderate threat level of the opposing starter. Meanwhile, **Kyle Tucker** (75.97) remains an elite volume play despite the matchup against Montero; his combination of Park (+5%) and SP Skill (+12%) adjustments underscores his role as the backbone of our outfield. **Jackson Merrill** (69.72) and **Drake Baldwin** (68.73) also draw strong marks, with the latter capitalizing on an optimal lead-off spot and a favorable matchup against the vulnerable Agnos, pushing his efficiency score deep into the high 60s.\n\nThe most challenging decision today involved the final outfield and utility slots, where **Miguel Vargas** (63.27) edged out **Luis Lara** (48.06). While **Luis Lara** offers versatile switch-hitting, his lower base projection and unfavorable position in the order pushed him firmly to the bench. **Miguel Vargas** earns the nod today due to his superior xwOBA metrics and a situational boost for his propensity to capitalize on the basepaths, which aligns with our prioritization of speed categories. We are proceeding with caution regarding **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** (49.47), whose score is suppressed by a negative platoon shift against Gamboa; he remains in the lineup only due to the relative lack of depth, but he is a candidate for early substitution if his metrics slip in real-time. Finally, note the benching of **Wade Meckler** (31.10), who falls below our 40.0 efficiency floor; facing the elite Cristopher S\u00e1nchez, the risk-to-reward ratio simply does not justify the use of a game cap.",
